# Copy to admin.env (gitignored) to run casebroker ADMIN commands without a
# password prompt -- setting the fleet's target build, registering a release,
# promoting a canary, rolling back.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `casebroker release target|register|promote|rollback`
# authenticates as a human admin and takes the password from a tty prompt or
# from --password-stdin; it keeps no session (the cookie jar is in memory and
# _admin_call logs out in a `finally`, deliberately -- see docs/operations.md).
# So an unattended or agent-driven run has nowhere to get the password, and the
# operation stalls on whoever is at the keyboard. scripts/broker-admin.sh reads
# this file and pipes the password in.
#
# WHAT IT IS WORTH. These are ADMIN credentials: they can change what every
# node in the fleet runs. Treat this file like an SSH private key. It is
# gitignored by name (.gitignore, beside machine.env) because a password in a
# tracked file is one `git add -A` from being published. Prefer a per-person
# admin account over a shared one, so revoking it costs one person.
#
# ON A CI RUNNER, do not use this file: pass the same two values as the
# repository secrets CASEBROKER_RELEASE_USER / CASEBROKER_RELEASE_PASSWORD and
# pipe them to --password-stdin, which is what Eddy3D's release workflow does.

# THE BROKER. The same host the nodes are paired with. A target set on the
# wrong broker silently moves nothing.
CASEBROKER_URL=https://casebroker.eddy3d.com

# THE ADMIN ACCOUNT. Same name you sign in to the dashboard with. The CLI
# refuses a non-admin with "changing what the fleet runs needs an admin", so a
# machine/worker token does NOT work here -- this is a human account.
#CASEBROKER_RELEASE_USER=you@example.org

# THE PASSWORD. No quotes needed; the whole line after `=` is the value, so a
# trailing space is part of the password. Never add this to a tracked file.
#CASEBROKER_RELEASE_PASSWORD=
